helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	br	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	br	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914				0	br	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	br	END SUB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	br	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397				0	br	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	br	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149404				0	br	As with variables, include a type-declaration character after the function name, or the type indicated by "As" and the corresponding key word at the end of the parameter list to define the type of the function's return value, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	br	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814				0	br	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited. This property is controlled in a variable declaration through key words in the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	br	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	br	The variable is valid in all modules.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208				0	br	DIM GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150368				0	br	The variable is only valid in this module.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	br	End Function				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	br	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150886				0	br	DIM VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114				0	br	Program code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215				0	br	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	br	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152899				0	br	Function WordCount(WordText as String) \<emph\>as Integer\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994				0	br	The variable is only valid in this module.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	br	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	br	DIM PUBLIC VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	br	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	br	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	br	The variable retains its value until the next time the FUNCTION or SUB is entered. The declaration must exist inside a SUB or a FUNCTION.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	br	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765				0	br	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	br	Program code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156288				0	br	DIM STATIC VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158212				0	br	DIM PRIVATE VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	br	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	br	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id4104129				0	br	' Now returns empty string				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6308786				0	br	initMyText				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id631733				0	br	End Sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6933500				0	br	print "in module1 : ", myText				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id6969512				0	br	'Option Explicit				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id7906125				0	br	' (or rises error for Option Explicit)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8055970				0	br	print "Now in module2 : ", myText				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8234199				0	br	REM ***** Module2 *****				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8738975				0	br	Enforce private variables to be private across modules by setting CompatibilityMode(true).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id9475997				0	br	myText = "Hello"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	br	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Using Procedures and Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147317				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020500.xhp\" name=\"Libraries, Modules and Dialogs\"\>Libraries, Modules and Dialogs\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575				0	br	Libraries				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149262				0	br	Modules				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152577				0	br	Dialog Modules				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	br	$[officename] Basic provides tools to help you structuring your projects. It supports various "units" which enable you to group individual SUBS and FUNCTIONS in a Basic project.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3147427				0	br	The following describes the basic use of libraries, modules and dialogs in $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377				0	br	Dialog modules contain dialog definitions, including the dialog box properties, the properties of each dialog element and the events assigned. Since a dialog module can only contain a single dialog, they are often referred to as "dialogs".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011				0	br	Libraries serve as a tool for organizing modules, and can either be attached to a document or a template. When the document or a template is saved, all modules contained in the library are automatically saved as well.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112				0	br	A library can contain up to 16,000 modules.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	br	A module contains SUBS and FUNCTIONS along with variable declarations. The length of the program that can be saved in a module is limited to 64 KB. If more space is required you can divide a $[officename] Basic project among several modules, and then save them in a single library.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Libraries, Modules and Dialogs				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145090				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>Basic IDE;Integrated Development Environment\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>IDE;Integrated Development Environment\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030000.xhp\" name=\"Integrated Development Environment (IDE)\"\>Integrated Development Environment (IDE)\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	par_id3146795				0	br	This section describes the Integrated Development Environment for $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Integrated Development Environment (IDE)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147291				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030100.xhp\" name=\"IDE Overview\"\>IDE Overview\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145787				0	br	The \<emph\>Call Stack\</emph\> window to the right provides information about the call stack of SUBS and FUNCTIONS when a program runs.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050000.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	br	In the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030200.xhp\" name=\"Editor window\"\>\<emph\>Editor window\</emph\>\</link\>, directly below the Macro toolbar, you can edit the Basic program code. The column on the left side is used to set breakpoints in the program code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686				0	br	The \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050100.xhp\" name=\"Watch\"\>\<emph\>Watch window\</emph\>\</link\> (observer) is located below the Editor window at the left, and displays the contents of variables or arrays during a single step process.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156344				0	br	The \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/main0211.xhp\" name=\"Macro Toolbar\"\>\<emph\>Macro Toolbar\</emph\>\</link\> in the IDE provides various icons for editing and testing programs.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	IDE Overview				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148647				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>saving;Basic code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic;loading and saving source code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>loading;Basic code\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>navigating;in Basic projects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>long lines;in Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>lines of text;in Basic editor\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>continuation;long lines in editor\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863				0	br	Navigating in a Project				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145785				0	br	The Library List				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147264				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030200.xhp\" name=\"The Basic Editor\"\>The Basic Editor\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148647				0	br	Saving and Loading Basic Source Code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149403				0	br	Saving Source Code to a Text File				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153190				0	br	The Object Catalog				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159264				0	br	Loading Source Code From a Text File				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069				0	br	The Basic Editor provides the standard editing functions you are familiar with when working in a text document. It supports the functions of the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> menu (Cut, Delete, Paste), the ability to select text with the Shift key, as well as cursor positioning functions (for example, moving from word to word with \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Option \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> and the arrow keys).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145230				0	br	Position the cursor where you want to insert the program code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	br	Select a library from the \<emph\>Library\</emph\> list at the left of the toolbar to load the library in the editor. The first module of the selected library will be displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	br	Select the module where you want to import the source code from the object catalog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565				0	br	Click the \<emph\>Insert Source Text\</emph\> icon in the Macro toolbar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149959				0	br	You cannot save Basic dialogs to a text file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	br	Select the module that you want to export as text from the object catalog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752				0	br	Click the \<emph\>Save Source As\</emph\> icon in the Macro toolbar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151042				0	br	If you press the \<emph\>Run BASIC\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> bar, program execution starts at the first line of the Basic editor. The "Sub Main" does not take precedence on program execution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01050000.xhp\" name=\"Basic IDE\"\>Basic IDE\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154020				0	br	Select the text file containing the source code and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320				0	br	You can save Basic code in a text file for saving and importing in other programming systems.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686				0	br	Long lines can be split into several parts by inserting a space and an underline character _ as the last two characters of a line. This connects the line with the following line to one logical line. (If "Option Compatible" is used in the same Basic module, the line continuation feature is also valid for comment lines.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	br	Select a file name and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\> to save the file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	par_id59816				0	br	Insert your Basic code between the Sub Main and End Sub lines that you see when you first open the IDE. Alternatively, delete all lines and then enter your own Basic code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	The Basic Editor				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153344				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>debugging Basic programs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic; debugging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; observing values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>watching variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>run-time errors in Basic\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>error codes in Basic\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>breakpoints\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Call Stack window\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148618				0	br	The Call Stack Window				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439				0	br	Observing the Value of Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150594				0	br	List of Run-Time Errors				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153344				0	br	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030300.xhp\"\>Debugging a Basic Program\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153825				0	br	Properties of a Breakpoint				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159224				0	br	Breakpoints and Single Step Execution				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	br	You can also include arrays in the Watch window. If you enter the name of an array variable without an index value in the Watch text box, the content of the entire array is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	br	If you rest the mouse over a predefined variable in the Editor at run-time, the content of the variable is displayed in a pop-up box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	br	The values of variables are only displayed if they are in scope. Variables that are not defined at the current source code location display ("Out of Scope") instead of a value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147303				0	br	Double-click in the \<emph\>breakpoint\</emph\> column at the left of the Editor window to toggle a breakpoint at the corresponding line. When the program reaches a breakpoint, the program execution is interrupted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147574				0	br	The properties of a breakpoint are available through its context menu by right-clicking the breakpoint in the breakpoint column.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3148473	10			0	br	You can \<emph\>activate\</emph\> and \<emph\>deactivate\</emph\> a breakpoint by selecting \<emph\>Active\</emph\> from its context menu. When a breakpoint is deactivated, it does not interrupt the program execution. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150682				0	br	You can check each line in your Basic program for errors using single step execution. Errors are easily traced since you can immediately see the result of each step. A pointer in the breakpoint column of the Editor indicates the current line. You can also set a breakpoint if you want to force the program to be interrupted at a specific position.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151110				0	br	The procedure step execution using the \<emph\>Procedure Step\</emph\> icon causes the program to skip over procedures and functions as a single step.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	br	You can monitor the values of a variable by adding it to the \<emph\>Watch\</emph\> window. To add a variable to the list of watched variables, type the variable name in the \<emph\>Watch\</emph\> text box and press Enter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	br	Click \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> to remove the breakpoint from the program.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154491				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_BASICIDE_STACKWINDOW_LIST\"\>Provides an overview of the call hierarchy of procedures and functions.\</ahelp\> You can determine which procedures and functions called which other procedures and functions at the current point in the source code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155805	8			0	br	The \<emph\>single step \</emph\>execution using the \<emph\>Single Step\</emph\> icon causes the program to branch into procedures and functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	br	The list displays all \<emph\>breakpoints\</emph\> with the corresponding line number in the source code. You can activate or deactivate a selected breakpoint by checking or clearing the \<emph\>Active\</emph\> box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	br	The \<emph\>Pass Count\</emph\> specifies the number of times the breakpoint can be passed over before the program is interrupted. If you enter 0 (default setting) the program is always interrupted as soon as a breakpoint is encountered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159413				0	br	Select \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> from the context menu of a breakpoint or select \<emph\>Breakpoints\</emph\> from the context menu of the breakpoint column to call the \<emph\>Breakpoints\</emph\> dialog where you can specify other breakpoint options.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Debugging a Basic Program				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148797				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>libraries;organizing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modules;organizing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying;modules\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>adding libraries\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>deleting;libraries/modules/dialogs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>dialogs;creating and renaming\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>moving;modules\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>organizing;modules/libraries\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>renaming modules\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125864				0	br	Creating a New Library				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146963				0	br	Deleting a Module or Dialog				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147070				0	br	Organizing Modules and Dialogs				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147394				0	br	Appending a Library				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148797				0	br	\<variable id=\"01030400\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030400.xhp\"\>Organizing Libraries and Modules\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150868				0	br	Organizing Libraries				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151392				0	br	Organizing Projects among Documents or Templates				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152872				0	br	Renaming a Module or Dialog				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155265				0	br	Creating a New Module or Dialog				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156400				0	br	Moving or copying modules between documents, templates and the application.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159100				0	br	Deleting a Library				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145228				0	br	Check the \<emph\>Replace existing libraries\</emph\> box if you want existing libraries of the same name to be overwritten.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145637				0	br	To move a module or dialog to another document, click the corresponding object in the list and drag it to the desired position. A horizontal line indicates the target position of the current object while dragging. Hold the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Option \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> key while dragging to copy the object instead of moving it.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	br	Select to where you want to append the library in the \<emph\>Location\</emph\> list. If you select %PRODUCTNAME Macros & Dialogs, the library will belong to the $[officename] application and will be available for all documents. If you select a document the library will be appended to this document and only available from there.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3146781				0	br	Click the \<emph\>Modules\</emph\> tab or the \<emph\>Dialogs\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
