helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155307				0	br	Start and End can be numeric expressions if ReDim is used at the procedure level.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	br	iVar(iCount) = iCount				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155601				0	br	In $[officename] Basic, you do not need to declare variables explicitly. However, you need to declare an array before you can use them. You can declare a variable with the Dim statement, using commas to separate multiple declarations. To declare a variable type, enter a type-declaration character following the name or use a corresponding key word.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156214				0	br	[ReDim]Dim VarName [(start To end)] [As VarType][, VarName2 [(start To end)] [As VarType][,...]]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423				0	br	\<emph\>VarName:\</emph\> Any variable or array name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	br	\<emph\>Double:\</emph\> Double floating point variable (1.79769313486232x10E308 - 4.94065645841247x10E-324)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
