helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148518				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MMULT\"\>Calculates the array product of two arrays.\</ahelp\> The number of columns for array 1 must match the number of rows for array 2. The square array has an equal number of rows and columns.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148679				0	br	If you want to edit the output array, do the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806				0	br	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148831				0	br	33				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148903				0	br	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148943				0	br	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149045				0	br	\<emph\>10\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149102				0	br	If you create an array formula using the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, you must mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box each time so that the results are returned in an array. Otherwise, only the value in the upper-left cell of the array being calculated is returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149156				0	br	In addition to multiplication, you can also use other operators on the reference range (an array). With $[officename] Calc, you can add (+), subtract (-), multiply (*), divide (/), use exponents (^), concatenation (&) and comparisons (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=). The operators can be used on each individual value in the cell range and return the result as an array if the array formula was entered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149174				0	br	\<emph\>3\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149241				0	br	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula. To select the whole array, position the cell cursor inside the array range, then press Ctrl+/, where / is the Division key on the numeric keypad.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149298				0	br	\<emph\>5\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149328				0	br	\<emph\>B\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149585				0	br	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149638				0	br	Select a square range and select MINVERSE. Select the output array, select the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> field and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149744				0	br	12				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149771				0	br	95				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787				0	br	Use array formulas if you have to repeat calculations using different values. If you decide to change the calculation method later, you only have to update the array formula. To add an array formula, select the entire array range and then \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp\" name=\"make the required change to the array formula\"\>make the required change to the array formula\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149798				0	br	array formulas are also a space saving option when several values must be calculated, since they are not very memory-intensive. In addition, arrays are an essential tool for carrying out complex calculations, because you can have several cell ranges included in your calculations. $[officename] has different math functions for arrays, such as the MMULT function for multiplying two arrays or the SUMPRODUCT function for calculating the scalar products of two arrays.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150117				0	br	B				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150217				0	br	33				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150245				0	br	15				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150290				0	br	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> represents a square array in which the determinants are defined.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150312				0	br	Select a single column range in which to enter the frequency for values that are greater than the upper class limit. You must select one field more than the class ceiling. In this example, select the range C1:C6. Call up the FREQUENCY function in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. Select the \<emph\>Data\</emph\> range in (A1:A11), and then the \<emph\>Classes\</emph\> range in which you entered the class limits (B1:B6). Select the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. You will see the frequency count in the range C1:C6.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150403				0	br	You can also enter the =Munit(5) formula in the last cell of the selected range (E5), and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Shift+Command+Enter \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Shift+Ctrl+Enter\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150529				0	br	31				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150628				0	br	You can format the separate parts of an array. For example, you can change the font color. Select a cell range and then change the attribute you want.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150779				0	br	5				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150798				0	br	MMULT(array; array)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150812				0	br	\<emph\>Array\</emph\> at first place represents the first array used in the array product.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949				0	br	Select a square range within the spreadsheet, for example, from A1 to E5.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150974				0	br	When you adjust the array range, the array formula will not automatically be adjusted. You are only changing the range in which the result will appear.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994				0	br	Copy the formula into the input line by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+C.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151007				0	br	\<emph\>7\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052				0	br	Not only can an array formula process several values, but it can also return several values. The results of an array formula is also an array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151067				0	br	20				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151102				0	br	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120				0	br	Array formulas appear in braces in $[officename] Calc. You cannot create array formulas by manually entering the braces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
