helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158058				0	br	\<emph\>Linear_Type\</emph\> and \<emph\>Stats\</emph\> are both set to 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158084				0	br	As soon as you click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>, $[officename] Calc will fill the above example with the LINEST values as shown in the example.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158106				0	br	The formula in the \<emph\>Formula\</emph\> Bar corresponds to each cell of the LINEST array {=LINEST(C2:C8;A2:B8;1;1)}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158128				0	br	\<emph\>This represents the calculated LINEST values:\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158146				0	br	E2 and F2: Slope \<emph\>m\</emph\> of the regression line y=b+m*x for the x1 and x2 values. The values are given in reverse order; that is, the slope for x2 in E2 and the slope for x1 in F2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158184				0	br	G2: Intersection b with the y axis.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158204				0	br	E3 and F3: The standard error of the slope value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158233				0	br	675,45				2002-02-02 02:02:02
