helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158379				0	br	120				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	br	17				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158432				0	br	To multiply the values in the individual cells by 10 in the above array, you do not need to apply a formula to each individual cell or value. Instead you just need to use a single array formula. Select a range of 3 x 3 cells on another part of the spreadsheet, enter the formula "=10*A1:C3" and confirm this entry using the key combination \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter. The result is a 3 x 3 array in which the individual values in the cell range (A1:C3) are multiplied by a factor of 10.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159194				0	br	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159218				0	br	\<emph\>2\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159291				0	br	Examine the following examples:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159366				0	br	In the spreadsheet, select the range in which the transposed array can appear. If the original array has n rows and m columns, your selected range must have at least m rows and n columns. Then enter the formula directly, select the original array and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Shift+Command+Enter \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Shift+Ctrl+Enter\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>. Or, if you are using the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box. The transposed array appears in the selected target range and is protected automatically against changes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390				0	br	\<emph\>Dimensions\</emph\> refers to the size of the array unit.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159427				0	br	100				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159460				0	br	4,17				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159483				0	br	-3,48				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159506				0	br	\<emph\>4\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159533				0	br	6				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159556				0	br	11				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159579				0	br	104				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159611				0	br	0,87				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163123				0	br	LOGEST(Data_Y; Data_X; Function_Type; Stats)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163137				0	br	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> represents the Y Data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163155				0	br	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> (optional) represents the X Data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163174				0	br	\<emph\>Function_Type\</emph\> (optional). If Function_Type = 0, functions in the form y = m^x will be calculated. Otherwise, y = b*m^x functions will be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163196				0	br	\<emph\>Stats\</emph\> (optional). If Stats=0, only the regression coefficient is calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163230				0	br	See LINEST. However, no square sum will be returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163314				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_SUMMENPRODUKT\"\>Multiplies corresponding elements in the given arrays, and returns the sum of those products.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163347				0	br	SUMPRODUCT(Array 1; Array 2...Array 30)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163362				0	br	\<emph\>Array 1, Array 2...Array 30\</emph\> represent arrays whose corresponding elements are to be multiplied.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163404				0	br	SUMX2PY2(Array X; Array Y)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163417				0	br	\<emph\>Array X\</emph\> represents the first array whose arguments are to be squared and added.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163437				0	br	\<emph\>Array Y\</emph\> represents the second array, whose elements are to be added and squared.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163556				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_SUMMEXMY2\"\>Adds the squares of the variance between corresponding values in two arrays.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163588				0	br	SUMXMY2(Array X; Array Y)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163601				0	br	\<emph\>Array X\</emph\> represents the first array whose elements are to be subtracted and squared.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163621				0	br	\<emph\>Array Y\</emph\> represents the second array, whose elements are to be subtracted and squared.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163724				0	br	20				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163734				0	br	y				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163766				0	br	LINEST value				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166091				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_TREND\"\>Returns values along a linear trend.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166122				0	br	TREND(Data_Y; Data_X; New Data_X; Linear_Type)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166137				0	br	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> represents the Y Data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166156				0	br	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> (optional) represents the X Data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166176				0	br	\<emph\>New Data_X\</emph\> (optional) represents the array of the X data, which are used for recalculating values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166196				0	br	\<emph\>Linear_Type\</emph\>(Optional). If \<emph\>Linear_Type\</emph\> = 0, then lines will be calculated through the zero point. Otherwise, offset lines will also be calculated. The default is \<emph\>Linear_Type\</emph\> <> 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166245				0	br	Select a spreadsheet range in which the trend data will appear. Select the function. Enter the output data or select it with the mouse. Mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> field. click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. The trend data calculated from the output data is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166346				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_VARIATION\"\>Calculates the points of an exponential trend in an array.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166377				0	br	Growth(Data_Y;Data_X; New_Data_X; Function_type)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166392				0	br	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> represents the Y Data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166411				0	br	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> (optional) represents the X Data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166456				0	br	When you change comparison operators in an array formula, empty cells follow the same rules as single comparisons; that is, it could be represented either by 0 or as an empty string. Therefore, the array formulas {=A1:A2=""} and {=A1:A2=0} will both return TRUE if cells A1 and A2 are empty.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3173797				0	br	\<emph\>New_Data_X\</emph\> (optional) represents the X data array, in which the values are recalculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3173817				0	br	\<emph\>Function_Type\</emph\>(optional). If Function_Type = 0, functions in the form y = m^x will be calculated. Otherwise, y = b*m^x functions will be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3173852				0	br	This function returns an array and is handled in the same way as the other array functions. Select a range where you want the answers to appear and select the function. Select Data_Y. Enter any other parameters, mark \<emph\>Array\</emph\> and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id4262520				0	br	\<emph\>Examples:\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id5262916				0	br	={0;1;2|FALSE;TRUE;"two"}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id6626483				0	br	An array with two rows and three values in each row.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id6757103				0	br	To enter this array constant, you select three cells in a row, then you type the formula \<item type=\"literal\"\>={1;2;3}\</item\> using the curly braces and the semicolons, then press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Ctrl+Shift+Enter\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id7781914				0	br	=SIN({1;2;3})				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id8207037				0	br	An array with one row consisting of the three numbers 1, 2, and 3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id8868068				0	br	={1;2;3|4;5;6}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id936613				0	br	The column separator (separating elements in one row) is the ';' semicolon. The row separator is a '|' pipe symbol. The separators are not language and locale dependent. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id9387493				0	br	={1;2;3}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id985747				0	br	Calc supports inline matrix/array constants in formulas. An inline array is surrounded by curly braces '{' and '}'. Elements can be each a number (including negatives), a logical constant (TRUE, FALSE), or a literal string. Non-constant expressions are not allowed. Arrays can be entered with one or more rows, and one or more columns. All rows must consist of the same number of elements, all columns must consist of the same number of elements. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B75				0	br	B (result)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10C9B				0	br	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D47				0	br	Conditional Array Calculations				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D4B				0	br	A conditional array calculation is an array or matrix formula that includes an IF() or CHOOSE() function. The condition argument in the formula is an area reference or a matrix result.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D4E				0	br	In the following example, the >0 test of the {=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")} formula is applied to each cell in the range A1:A3 and the result is copied to the corresponding cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D65				0	br	A				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D6B				0	br	B (formula)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D71				0	br	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
