helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153260	51			0	br	\<emph\>reference\</emph\> is a cell reference, entered either directly or by specifying a range name. If the reference consists of multiple ranges, you must enclose the reference or range name in parentheses.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153277	198			0	br	{=COLUMN(Rabbit)} returns the single-row array (3, 4) if "Rabbit" is the named area (C1:D3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153298				0	br	1: absolute ($A$1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153334				0	br	4: relative (A1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153389				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_VERWEIS\"\>Returns the contents of a cell either from a one-row or one-column range or from an array.\</ahelp\> Optionally, the assigned value (of the same index) is returned in a different column and row. As opposed to \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060109.xhp\" name=\"VLOOKUP\"\>VLOOKUP\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060109.xhp\" name=\"HLOOKUP\"\>HLOOKUP\</link\>, search and result vector may be at different positions; they do not have to be adjacent. Additionally, the search vector for the LOOKUP must be sorted, otherwise the search will not return any usable results.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153671	209			0	br	=ROW() returns 3 if the formula was entered in row 3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153701	207			0	br	=ROW(D5:D8) returns 5 because the ROW function is not used as array formula and only the number of the first row of the reference is returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153739	122			0	br	=SUM(OFFSET(A1; 2; 2; 5; 6)) determines the total of the area that starts in cell C3 and has a height of 5 rows and a width of 6 columns (area=C3:H7).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153775				0	br	Data are retrieved as text; no conversion to numbers				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153790	210			0	br	{=ROW(Rabbit)} returns the single-column array (1, 2, 3) if "Rabbit" is the named area (C1:D3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153884	92			0	br	\<emph\>array\</emph\> is the reference, which is to comprise at least two columns.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3153962	14			0	br	\<emph\>sheet\</emph\> represents the name of the sheet. It must be placed in double quotes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154047				0	br	COLUMNS(array)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154104				0	br	LOOKUP(Search criterion;Search vector;result_vector)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154129				0	br	You want to enter the number of a dish on the menu in cell A1, and the name of the dish is to appear as text in the neighboring cell (B1) immediately. The Number to Name assignment is contained in the D1:E100 array. D1 contains 100, E1 contains the name \<emph\>Vegetable Soup\</emph\>, and so forth, for 100 menu items. The numbers in column D are sorted in ascending order; thus, the optional \<emph\>Sort order\</emph\> parameter is not necessary.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154265				0	br	If Type = 0, only exact matches are found. If the search criterion is found more than once, the function returns the first one found. Only if Type = 0 can you search for regular expressions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154317	66			0	br	\<emph\>ref\</emph\> represents a reference to a cell or an area (in text form) for which to return the contents.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154357				0	br	ROWS(array)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154380				0	br	Data are always interpreted in the standard format for US English				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154383	185			0	br	\<emph\>Mode\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154558				0	br	0 or missing				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154564				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ZEILE\"\>Returns the row number of a cell reference.\</ahelp\> If the reference is a cell, it returns the row number of the cell. If the reference is a cell range, it returns the corresponding row numbers in a one-column \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp#wasmatrix\" name=\"Array\"\>Array\</link\> if the formula is entered \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp#somatrixformel\" name=\"as an array formula\"\>as an array formula\</link\>. If the ROW function with a range reference is not used in an array formula, only the row number of the first range cell will be returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154588	219			0	br	\<emph\>Reference\</emph\> is optional and is the reference to a cell, an area, or a sheet name string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154628	53			0	br	\<emph\>column\</emph\> (optional) represents the column number of the reference range, for which to return a value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154707				0	br	ADDRESS(row; column; abs;sheet)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154725	212			0	br	=Rows(B5) returns 1 because a cell only contains one row.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154745	83			0	br	\<emph\>array\</emph\> is the reference to a cell range whose total number of columns is to be found. The argument can also be a single cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154830	205			0	br	=ROW(B3) returns 3 because the reference refers to the third row in the table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154842	32			0	br	\<emph\>Server\</emph\> is the name of a server application. \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> applications have the server name "Soffice".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154854	128			0	br	\<emph\>Search vector\</emph\> is the single-row or single-column area to be searched.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154896				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_VERGLEICH\"\>Returns the relative position of an item in an array that matches a specified value.\</ahelp\> The function returns the position of the value found in the lookup_array as a number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3154916				0	br	ROW(reference)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155143	213			0	br	=ROWS(Rabbit) returns 3 if "Rabbit" is the named area (C1:D3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155343	109			0	br	=MATCH(200; D1:D100) searches the area D1:D100, which is sorted by column D, for the value 200. As soon as this value is reached, the number of the row in which it was found is returned. If a higher value is found during the search in the column, the number of the previous row is returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155425				0	br	CHOOSE(Index; value1;...value30)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155514	54			0	br	\<emph\>range\</emph\> (optional) represents the index of the subrange if referring to a multiple range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155762				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ADRESSE\"\>Returns a cell address (reference) as text, according to the specified row and column numbers.\</ahelp\> You can determine whether the address is interpreted as an absolute address (for example, $A$1) or as a relative address (as A1) or in a mixed form (A$1 or $A1). You can also specify the name of the sheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155837				0	br	If no reference is entered, the column number of the cell in which the formula is entered is found. \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc automatically sets the reference to the current cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155846	201			0	br	=COLUMNS(Rabbit) returns 2 if "Rabbit" is the named range (C1:D3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155907				0	br	AREAS(Reference)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3155942	170			0	br	\<emph\>array\</emph\> is the reference or named area whose total number of rows is to be determined.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3156005	93			0	br	\<emph\>index\</emph\> is the number of the column in the array that contains the value to be returned. The first column has the number 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3156032	116			0	br	\<emph\>Rows\</emph\> is the number of cells by which the reference was corrected up (negative value) or down.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3156149	138			0	br	\<emph\>Time\</emph\> is an optional time range in seconds. If this parameter is missing the style will not be changed after a certain amount of time has passed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3156310	74			0	br	\<emph\>Reference\</emph\> is the reference to a cell or cell area whose first column number is to be found.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3156320	196			0	br	{=COLUMN(B2:B7)} and =COLUMN(B2:B7) both return 2 because the reference only contains column B as the second column in the table. Because single-column areas have only one column number, it does not make a difference whether or not the formula is used as an array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3156336	161			0	br	\<emph\>Reference\</emph\> is a cell, an area, or the name of an area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3158419	58			0	br	=INDEX((multi);4;1) indicates the value contained in row 4 and column 1 of the (multiple) range, which you named under \<emph\>Insert - Names - Set\</emph\> as \<emph\>multi\</emph\>. The multiple range may consist of several rectangular ranges, each with a row 4 and column 1. If you now want to call the second block of this multiple range enter the number 2 as the \<emph\>range\</emph\> parameter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3159112	56			0	br	=INDEX(Prices;4;1) returns the value from row 4 and column 1 of the database range defined in \<emph\>Data - Define\</emph\> as \<emph\>Prices\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3159152				0	br	MATCH(search criterion;lookup_array;type)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3159167	106			0	br	\<emph\>lookup_array\</emph\> is the reference searched. A lookup array can be a single row or column, or part of a single row or column.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3159194				0	br	OFFSET(reference;rows;columns;height;width)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3159260				0	br	If the cell A1 in sheet 2 contains the value -6, you can refer indirectly to the referenced cell using a function in B2 by entering =ABS(INDIRECT(B2)). The result is the absolute value of the cell reference specified in B2, which in this case is 6.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3166458	117			0	br	\<emph\>Columns\</emph\> is the number of columns by which the reference was corrected to the left (negative value) or to the right.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3168736				0	br	A function name can be added in the form \<emph\>Field[Item;Function]\</emph\>, which will cause the constraint to match only subtotal values which use that function. The possible function names are Sum, Count, Average, Max, Min, Product, CountNums, StDev, StDevp, Var, and Varp, case-insensitive.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3593859				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The GETPIVOTDATA function returns a result value from a DataPilot table. The value is addressed using field and item names, so it remains valid if the layout of the DataPilot table changes.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id3864253				0	br	Subtotal values from the DataPilot table are only used if they use the function "auto" (except when specified in the constraint, see \<item type=\"literal\"\>Second Syntax\</item\> below).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id4076357				0	br	One of the entries can be the data field name. The data field name can be left out if the DataPilot table contains only one data field, otherwise it must be present. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id4484084				0	br	If LOOKUP cannot find the search criterion, it matches the largest value in the search vector that is less than or equal to the search criterion.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id4809411				0	br	If no \<emph\>Field n / Item n\</emph\> pairs are given, the grand total is returned. Otherwise, each pair adds a constraint that the result must satisfy. \<emph\>Field n\</emph\> is the name of a field from the DataPilot table. \<emph\>Item n\</emph\> is the name of an item from that field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id4997100				0	br	GETPIVOTDATA(DataPilot; Constraints)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id5616626				0	br	\<emph\>Constraints\</emph\> is a space-separated list. Entries can be quoted (single quotes). The whole string must be enclosed in quotes (double quotes), unless you reference the string from another cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id6454969				0	br	If the DataPilot table contains only a single result value that fulfills all of the constraints, or a subtotal result that summarizes all matching values, that result is returned. If there is no matching result, or several ones without a subtotal for them, an error is returned. These conditions apply to results that are included in the DataPilot table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id79042				0	br	If the source data contains entries that are hidden by settings of the DataPilot table, they are ignored. The order of the Field/Item pairs is not significant. Field and item names are not case-sensitive.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id7928708				0	br	If no constraint for a page field is given, the field's selected value is implicitly used. If a constraint for a page field is given, it must match the field's selected value, or an error is returned. Page fields are the fields at the top left of a DataPilot table, populated using the "Page Fields" area of the DataPilot Layout dialog. From each page field, an item (value) can be selected, which means only that item is included in the calculation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id8231757				0	br	Each of the other entries specifies a constraint in the form \<item type=\"literal\"\>Field[Item]\</item\> (with literal characters [ and ]), or only \<item type=\"literal\"\>Item\</item\> if the item name is unique within all fields that are used in the DataPilot table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id8296151				0	br	\<emph\>DataPilot\</emph\> is a reference to a cell or cell range that is positioned within a DataPilot table or contains a DataPilot table. If the cell range contains several DataPilot tables, the table that was created last is used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id909451				0	br	Two different syntax definitions can be used:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id9302346				0	br	\<emph\>Target Field\</emph\> is a string that selects one of the DataPilot table's data fields. The string can be the name of the source column, or the data field name as shown in the table (like "Sum - Sales").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_id9937131				0	br	\<emph\>DataPilot\</emph\> has the same meaning as in the first syntax.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN10CAE				0	br	If you open an Excel spreadsheet that uses indirect addresses calculated from string functions, the sheet addresses will not be translated automatically. For example, the Excel address in INDIRECT("filename!sheetname"&B1) is not converted into the Calc address in INDIRECT("filename.sheetname"&B1).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN1104B				0	br	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN111CA				0	br	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11798				0	br	HYPERLINK				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN117F1				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HYPERLINK\"\>When you click a cell that contains the HYPERLINK function, the hyperlink opens.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11800				0	br	If you use the optional \<emph\>cell text\</emph\> parameter, the formula locates the URL, and then displays the text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11803				0	br	To open a hyperlinked cell with the keyboard, select the cell, press F2 to enter the Edit mode, move the cursor in front of the hyperlink, press Shift+F10, and then choose \<emph\>Open Hyperlink\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN1180A				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN1180E				0	br	HYPERLINK (URL) or HYPERLINK (URL;cell text)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11811				0	br	\<emph\>URL\</emph\> specifies the link target. The optional \<emph\>cell text\</emph\> parameter is the text that is displayed in the cell and the result of the function. If the \<emph\>cell text\</emph\> parameter is not specified, the \<emph\>URL\</emph\> is displayed in the cell text and in the result of the function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11823				0	br	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11827				0	br	=HYPERLINK("http://www.example.org") displays the text "http://www.example.org" in the cell and executes the hyperlink http://www.example.org when clicked.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN1182A				0	br	=HYPERLINK("http://www.example.org";"Click here") displays the text "Click here" in the cell and executes the hyperlink http://www.example.org when clicked.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN1182D				0	br	=HYPERLINK($B4) where cell B4 contains "http://www.example.org". The function adds "http://www.example.org" to the URL of the hyperlink cell and returns the same text which is used as formula result.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	par_idN11830				0	br	=HYPERLINK("http://www.";"Click ") & "example.org" displays the text "Click example.org" in the cell and executes the hyperlink http://www.example.org when clicked.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060109.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Spreadsheet Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060110.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145166				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>DECIMAL function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060110.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145178				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>UPPER function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060110.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145226				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>hexadecimal system; converting to\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060110.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145389				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions; text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Function Wizard;text\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060110.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147083				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>LEFT function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
