helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153756	111			0	br	=ZTEST(A1:A50; 12) yields the probability that value 12 belongs to the standard distribution of the total population of data in A1:A50.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153914	7			0	br	\<emph\>degrees_freedom_1\</emph\> is the number of degrees of freedom in the numerator of the F distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3153932				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GAMMAINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the Gamma cumulative distribution.\</ahelp\> This function allows you to search for variables with different distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154303	117			0	br	\<emph\>Number 1,Number 2,...Number 30\</emph\> are up to 30 values or ranges, that can be used to calculate the harmonic mean.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154502	61			0	br	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the value for which the natural logarithm of the Gamma function is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154561	53			0	br	\<emph\>Beta\</emph\> is the parameter Beta of the Gamma distribution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154734				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_FISHERINV\"\>Returns the inverse of the Fisher transformation for x and creates a function close to a normal distribution.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154740	109			0	br	\<emph\>Sigma\</emph\> (optional) is the standard deviation of the total population. If this argument is missing, the standard deviation of the sample in question will be processed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154821				0	br	TRIMMEAN(Data; Alpha)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154867	82			0	br	GAUSS(0.19) = 0.08				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154904	131			0	br	=HYPGEOMDIST(2; 2; 90; 100) yields 0.81. If 90 out of 100 pieces of buttered toast fall from the table and hit the floor with the buttered side first, then if 2 pieces of buttered toast are dropped from the table, the probability is 81%, that both will strike buttered side first.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060182.xhp	0	help	par_id3154933	126			0	br	\<emph\>X\</emph\> is the number of results achieved in the random sample.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
