helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	par_id3156106				0	br	TODAY() returns the current computer system date.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_today.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	TODAY				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154925				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>WEEKDAY function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147217				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153836				0	br	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154925				0	br	\<variable id=\"weekday\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_weekday.xhp\"\>WEEKDAY\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3149033				0	br	WEEKDAY(Number; Type)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3149046				0	br	\<emph\>Number\</emph\>, as a date value, is a decimal for which the weekday is to be returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3150317				0	br	WEEKDAY("6/14/2000") returns 4 (the Type parameter is missing, therefore the standard count is used. The standard count starts with Sunday as day number 1. June 14, 2000 was a Wednesday and therefore day number 4).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3150575				0	br	WEEKDAY(NOW()) returns the number of the current day.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3150588	171			0	br	To obtain a function indicating whether a day in A1 is a business day, use the IF and WEEKDAY functions as follows: IF(WEEKDAY(A1;2)<6;"Business day";"Weekend")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3153174				0	br	WEEKDAY("7/24/1996";2) returns 3 (the Type parameter is 2, therefore Monday is day number 1. July 24, 1996 was a Wednesday and therefore day number 3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3153525				0	br	WEEKDAY("7/24/1996";1) returns 4 (the Type parameter is 1, therefore Sunday is day number 1. July 24, 1996 was a Wednesday and therefore day number 4).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3154228				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_WOCHENTAG\"\>Returns the day of the week for the given date value.\</ahelp\> The day is returned as an integer between 1 (Sunday) and 7 (Saturday) if no type or type = 1 is specified. If type=2, numbering begins at Monday=1; and if type=3 numbering begins at Monday=0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3154394				0	br	\<emph\>Type\</emph\> determines the type of calculation. For Type =1, the weekdays are counted starting from Sunday (this is the default even when the Type parameter is missing). For Type =2, the weekdays are counted starting from Monday =1. For Type = 3, the weekdays are counted starting from Monday = 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	par_id3156188				0	br	These values apply only to the standard date format that you select under \<emph\>Tools - Options - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Calculate\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weekday.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	WEEKDAY 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159161				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>WEEKNUM function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146948				0	br	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153055				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159161				0	br	\<variable id=\"weeknum\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_weeknum.xhp\"\>WEEKNUM\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3147236				0	br	WEEKNUM(number; mode)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3147511				0	br	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> is the internal date number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3148930				0	br	1 = Sunday				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3149770				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_KALENDERWOCHE\"\>WEEKNUM calculates the week number of the year for the internal date value.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3149792				0	br	WEEKNUM("1/1/95";2) returns 52. If the week starts on Monday, Sunday belongs to the last week of the previous year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3150704				0	br	WEEKNUM("1/1/95";1) returns 1 (1/1/95 was a Sunday)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3154269				0	br	\<emph\>Mode\</emph\> sets the start of the week and the calculation type.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_id3154280				0	br	2 = Monday				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	par_idN105E4				0	br	The International Standard ISO 8601 has decreed that Monday shall be the first day of the week. A week that lies partly in one year and partly in another is assigned a number in the year in which most of its days lie. That means that week number 1 of any year is the week that contains the January 4\<emph\>th\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknum.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	WEEKNUM				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	bm_id3166443				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>WEEKNUM_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152886				0	br	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153745				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166443				0	br	\<variable id=\"weeknumadd\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_weeknumadd.xhp\"\>WEEKNUM_ADD\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_id3149914				0	br	=WEEKNUM_ADD("24.12.2001";1) returns 52.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_id3149973				0	br	In which week number does 12.24.2001 fall?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_id3152945				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_WEEKNUM\"\>The result indicates the number of the calendar week for a \<emph\>Date\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_id3153685				0	br	WEEKNUM_ADD(Date;Return type)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_id3154098				0	br	\<emph\>Return type\</emph\>: 1 for week beginning on a Sunday, 2 for week beginning on a Monday.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_id3159277				0	br	\<emph\>Date\</emph\>: the date within the calendar week.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	par_idN105DD				0	br	The WEEKNUM_ADD function is designed to calculate week numbers exactly as Microsoft Excel does. Use the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_weeknum.xhp\"\>WEEKNUM\</link\> function, or format your date cells using the WW formatting code, when you need ISO 8601 week numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_weeknumadd.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	WEEKNUM_ADD 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149012				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>WORKDAY function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146944				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149012				0	br	\<variable id=\"workday\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_workday.xhp\"\>WORKDAY\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150141				0	br	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3146142				0	br	=WORKDAY(C3;D3;F3:J3) returns 12/28/2001. Format the serial date number as a date.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3147469				0	br	\<emph\>Start date\</emph\>: the date from when the calculation is carried out. If the start date is a workday, the day is included in the calculation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3149893				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_WORKDAY\"\> The result is a date number that can be formatted as a date. You then see the date of a day that is a certain number of \<emph\>Workdays\</emph\> away from the \<emph\>Start date\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3150693				0	br	\<emph\>Holidays\</emph\>: list of optional holidays. These are non-working days. Enter a cell range in which the holidays are listed individually.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3152782				0	br	What date comes 17 workdays after 1 December 2001? Enter the start date "12/1/2001" in C3 and the number of workdays in D3. Cells F3 to J3 contain the following Christmas and New Year holidays: "12/24/2001", "12/25/2001", "12/26/2001", "12/31/2001", "1/1/2002".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3153038				0	br	\<emph\>Days\</emph\>: the number of workdays. Positive value for a result after the start date, negative value for a result before the start date.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	par_id3154844				0	br	WORKDAY (Start date;Days;Holidays)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_workday.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	WORKDAY				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153982				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>YEAR function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146090				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152797				0	br	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153982				0	br	\<variable id=\"year\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_year.xhp\"\>YEAR\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	par_id3145668				0	br	Year(1) returns 1899				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	par_id3147496				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_JAHR\"\>Returns the year as a number according to the internal calculation rules.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	par_id3150115				0	br	YEAR(33333.33) returns 1991				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	par_id3151168				0	br	YEAR(2) returns 1900				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304				0	br	YEAR(number)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	par_id3156013				0	br	\<emph\>Number\</emph\> shows the internal date value for which the year is to be returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_year.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	YEAR				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148735				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>YEARFRAC function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145626				0	br	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148735				0	br	\<variable id=\"yearfrac\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_yearfrac.xhp\"\>YEARFRAC\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155259				0	br	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3145144				0	br	\<emph\>Start date\</emph\> and \<emph\>end date\</emph\>: two date values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3145245				0	br	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3145297				0	br	3				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3146847				0	br	Basis				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3148394				0	br	Exact number of days in month, year has 365 days				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3148766				0	br	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3149007				0	br	What fraction of the year 2001 lies between 1.1.2002 and 7.1.2001?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3149877				0	br	US method (NASD), 12 months of 30 days each				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3149954				0	br	\<emph\>Basis\</emph\>: is chosen from a list of options and indicates how the year is to be calculated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3150899				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_YEARFRAC\"\> The result is a number between 0 and 1, representing the fraction of a year between \<emph\>Start date\</emph\> and \<emph\>End date\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3150931				0	br	European method, 12 months of 30 days each				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3151022				0	br	4				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3154326				0	br	Exact number of days in months, exact number of days in year				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3154502				0	br	0 or missing				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3154632				0	br	=YEARFRAC("1.1.2002"; "7.1.2002";1) returns 0.495890.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3155620				0	br	Exact number of days in month, year has 360 days				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3155823				0	br	YEARFRAC (Start date;End date;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	par_id3155956				0	br	Calculation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_yearfrac.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	YEARFRAC				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\solver_options.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Options				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	bm_id8004394				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>text to columns\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id2300180				0	br	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/text2columns.xhp\"\>Text to Columns\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id9599597				0	br	To expand cell contents to multiple cells				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id1517380				0	br	You can select or enter separator characters to define the positions of breaking points. The separator characters are removed from the resulting cell contents.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2021546				0	br	You can expand cells that contain comma separated values (CSV) into multiple cells in the same row. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2623981				0	br	For example, cell A1 contains the comma separated values \<item type=\"literal\"\>1,2,3,4\</item\>, and cell A2 contains the text \<item type=\"literal\"\>A,B,C,D\</item\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6334116				0	br	You see the Text to Columns dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id655232				0	br	\<variable id=\"text2columns\"\>Opens the Text to Columns dialog, where you enter settings to expand the contents of selected cells to multiple cells. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6999420				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Data - Text to Columns\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7110812				0	br	In the example, you select the comma as a delimiter character. Cells A1 and B1 will be expanded to four columns. A1 contains 1, A2 contains 2, and so on.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7242042				0	br	Select the cell or cells that you want to expand.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id8523819				0	br	You can select a fixed width and then click the ruler on the preview to set cell breakup positions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id9276406				0	br	Select the separator options. The preview shows how the current cell contents will be transformed into multiple cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Text to Columns				2002-02-02 02:02:02
