helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146120				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>dates; in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>times; in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells;date and time formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>current date and time values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146120				0	br	\<variable id=\"calc_date\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_date.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Dates and Times\"\>Calculating With Dates and Times\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3145642	14			0	br	Enter the following formula in cell A3: =NOW()-A1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001				0	br	The number of days between today's date and the specified date is displayed in cell A3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	br	The \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog appears. On the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab, the \<emph\>Number\</emph\> category will appear already highlighted. The format is set to "General", which causes the result of a calculation containing date entries to be displayed as a date. To display the result as a number, set the number format to "-1,234" and close the dialog with the OK button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020				0	br	After pressing the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key you will see the result in date format. Since the result should show the difference between two dates as a number of days, you must format cell A3 as a number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149207				0	br	The time since your date of birth will be calculated and displayed in the various units. The values are calculated as of the exact moment when you entered the last formula and pressed the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key. This value is not automatically updated, although "Now" continuously changes. In the \<emph\>Tools\</emph\> menu, the menu item \<emph\>Cell Contents - AutoCalculate\</emph\> is normally active; however, automatic calculation does not apply to the function NOW. This ensures that your computer is not solely occupied with updating the sheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	br	Experiment with some additional formulas: in A4 enter =A3*24 to calculate the hours, in A5 enter =A4*60 for the minutes, and in A6 enter =A5*60 for seconds. Press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key after each formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150750				0	br	In a spreadsheet, enter your birthday in cell A1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320				0	br	In $[officename] Calc, you can perform calculations with current date and time values. As an example, to find out exactly how old you are in seconds or hours, follow the following steps:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	br	Place the cursor in cell A3, right-click to open a context menu and choose \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Calculating With Dates and Times				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>series; calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>linear series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>growth series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>date series\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>powers of 2 calculations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; filling automatically\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>auto filling cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>AutoFill;cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filling;cells, automatically\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769				0	br	\<variable id=\"calc_series\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\" name=\"Automatically Calculating Series\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	br	Drag the fill handle in the bottom right corner of the cell across the cells that you want to fill, and release the mouse button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801				0	br	The cells are filled with ascending numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	br	Select the cell range in the sheet that you want to fill.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	br	On a sheet, click in cell, and type a number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	br	If you select two or more adjacent cells that contain different numbers, and drag, the remaining cells are filled with the arithmetic pattern that is recognized in the numbers. The AutoFill function also recognizes customized lists that are defined under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Options - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Fill - Series\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id3159173				0	br	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_id9720145				0	br	Hold down Ctrl if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A8				0	br	You can automatically fill cells with data with the AutoFill command or the Series command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CB				0	br	Click in another cell and then click back in the cell where you typed the number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D3				0	br	Using AutoFill				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D7				0	br	AutoFill automatically generates a data series based on a defined pattern.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EE				0	br	To quickly create a list of consecutive days, enter "Monday" in a cell, and drag the fill handle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10713				0	br	Using a Defined Series				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10716				0	br	Select the parameters for the series. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10731				0	br	If you select a \<emph\>linear\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>added\</emph\> to each consecutive number in the series to create the next value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10737				0	br	You can double-click the fill handle to automatically fill all empty columns of the current data block. For example, first enter Jan into A1 and drag the fill handle down to A12 to get the twelve months in the first column. Now enter some values into B1 and C1. Select those two cells, and double-click the fill handle. This fills automatically the data block B1:C12.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN1073C				0	br	If you select a \<emph\>growth\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is \<emph\>multiplied\</emph\> by each consecutive number to create the next value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	par_idN10747				0	br	If you select a \<emph\>date\</emph\> series, the increment that you enter is added to the time unit that you specify.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_series.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Automatically Calculating Series				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;time differences\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>time differences\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769				0	br	\<variable id=\"calc_timevalues\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_timevalues.xhp\" name=\"Calculating Time Differences\"\>Calculating Time Differences\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3145271				0	br	In the formula, an entire 24-hour day has a value of 1 and one hour has a value of 1/24. The logical value in parentheses is 0 or 1, corresponding to 0 or 24 hours. The result returned by the formula is automatically issued in time format due to the sequence of the operands.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3149263				0	br	If you want to calculate time differences, for example, the time between 23:30 and 01:10 in the same night, use the following formula:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3152598				0	br	The later time is B2 and the earlier time is A2. The result of the example is 01:40 or 1 hour and 40 minutes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153				0	br	=(B2<A2)+B2-A2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Calculating Time Differences				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; calculating\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791	25			0	br	\<variable id=\"calculate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calculate.xhp\" name=\"Calculating in Spreadsheets\"\>Calculating in Spreadsheets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120	6			0	br	The following is an example of a calculation in $[officename] Calc.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343	20			0	br	The result of the formula appears in the cell. If you want, you can edit the formula in the input line of the Formula bar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951	7			0	br	Click in a cell, and type a number				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253	10			0	br	Type in a formula, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A3 * A4 / 100.\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064	9			0	br	Enter another number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155378	12			0	br	When you edit a formula, the new result is calculated automatically.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10656				0	br	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	br	The cursor moves down to the next cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066F				0	br	Press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10676				0	br	The cursor moves to the right into the next cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1068B				0	br	 Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Calculating in Spreadsheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150868				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>values; inserting in multiple cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges;selecting for data entries\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>areas, see also cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id1867427				0	br	Area detection for new rows				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3405255				0	br	\<variable id=\"cell_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_enter.xhp\"\>Entering Values\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3583788				0	br	Area selection				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id5621509				0	br	To enter values into a range of cells manually				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id8950163				0	br	To enter values to a range of cells automatically				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id2011780				0	br	Use drag-and-drop to select the area where you want to input values. But start dragging from the last cell of the area and release the mouse button when you have selected the first cell. Now you can start to input values. Always press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> key to advance to the next cell. You will not leave the selected area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3232520				0	br	Select the area from E7 to B3. Now B3 is waiting for your input. Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> to advance to the next cell within the selected area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id6196783				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id6473586\" src=\"res/helpimg/area1.png\" width=\"6.4437inch\" height=\"2.1252inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id6473586\"\>area detection\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id633869				0	br	See \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7044282				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id2811365\" src=\"res/helpimg/area2.png\" width=\"6.4437inch\" height=\"2.1252inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id2811365\"\>area selection\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7147129				0	br	Calc can simplify entering data and values into multiple cells. You can change some settings to conform to your preferences.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7908871				0	br	In the row below a heading row, you can advance from one cell to the next with the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> key. After you enter the value into the last cell in the current row, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\>. Calc positions the cursor below the first cell of the current block.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8118839				0	br	In row 3, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> to advance from cell B3 to C3, D3, and E3. Then press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> to advance to B4.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8200018				0	br	There are two features that assist you when you enter a block of data manually.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Entering Values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146119				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>protecting;cells and sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; protecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell protection; enabling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; protecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>documents; protecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; hiding for printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modifying; sheet protection\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing; document protection\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hiding;formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;hiding\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146119				0	br	\<variable id=\"cell_protect\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_protect.xhp\" name=\"Protecting Cells from Changes\"\>Protecting Cells from Changes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145261				0	br	Protection can be provided by means of a password, but it does not have to be. If you have assigned a password, protection can only be removed once the correct password has been entered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145362				0	br	Apply the protection options.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	br	Note that the cell protection for cells with the \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> attribute is only effective when you protect the whole table. In the default condition, every cell has the \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> attribute. Therefore you must remove the attribute selectively for those cells where the user may make changes. You then protect the whole table and save the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3149019				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Cells\</item\> and click the \<emph\>Cell Protection\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152872				0	br	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152898	10			0	br	Select \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> to prevent changes to the contents and the format of a cell. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985				0	br	Select the protection options that you want.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	br	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc you can protect sheets and the document as a whole. You can choose whether the cells are protected against accidental changes, whether the formulas can be viewed from within Calc, whether the cells are visible or whether the cells can be printed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	13			0	br	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id5974303				0	br	These protection features are just switches to prevent accidential action. The features are not intended to provide any secure protection. For example, by exporting a sheet to another file format, a user may be able to surpass the protection features. There is only one secure protection: the password that you can apply when saving an OpenDocument file. A file that has been saved with a password can be opened only with the same password.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066B				0	br	Select the cells that you want to specify the cell protection options for.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN1069A				0	br	Select \<emph\>Hide formula\</emph\> to hide and to protect formulas from changes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A1				0	br	Select \<emph\>Hide when printing\</emph\> to hide protected cells in the printed document. The cells are not hidden onscreen.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106C0				0	br	To protect the cells from being changed / viewed / printed according to your settings in the \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\> dialog, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Protect Document - Sheet\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106C7				0	br	To protect the structure of the document, for example the count, \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/rename_table.xhp\"\>names\</link\>, and order of the sheets, from being changed, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Protect Document - Document\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CF				0	br	(Optional) Enter a password.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D2				0	br	If you forget your password, you cannot deactivate the protection. If you only want to protect cells from accidental changes, set the sheet protection, but do not enter a password.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Protecting Cells from Changes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153252				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>cell protection; unprotecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>protecting; unprotecting cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>unprotecting cells\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153252	14			0	br	\<variable id=\"cell_unprotect\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_unprotect.xhp\" name=\"Unprotecting Cells\"\>Unprotecting Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	br	If you have assigned a password, enter it in this dialog and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3149656				0	br	Select \<emph\>Tools - Protect Document\</emph\>, then choose \<emph\>Sheet\</emph\> or \<emph\>Document\</emph\> to remove the check mark indicating the protected status.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112				0	br	Click the sheet for which you want to cancel the protection.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771				0	br	The cells can now be edited, the formulas can be viewed, and all cells can be printed until you reactivate the protection for the sheet or document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_unprotect.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Unprotecting Cells				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellcopy.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150440				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>cells; copying, deleting, formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rows;visible and invisible\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>moving;visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>deleting;visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>invisible cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>filters;copying visible cells only\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hidden cells\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
