helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_view.xhp	0	help	par_id3153726				0	br	Under the menu item \<emph\>Tools - Options - %PRODUCTNAME Calc\</emph\>\<emph\>,\</emph\> go to the \<emph\>View\</emph\> tab page. Unmark \<emph\>Grid lines\</emph\>. Confirm with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_view.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	br	Under the menu item \<emph\>Tools - Options - %PRODUCTNAME Calc,\</emph\> go to the \<emph\>View\</emph\> tab page. Unmark\<emph\> Column/row headers\</emph\>. Confirm with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_view.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Changing Table Views				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>numbers; as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text formats; for numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>numbers; entering without number formats \</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formats; numbers as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell formats; text/numbers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;numbers as text\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	br	\<variable id=\"text_numbers\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_numbers.xhp\" name=\"Formatting Numbers as Text\"\>Formatting Numbers as Text\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3144765				0	br	If you decide to enter a number directly as text, enter an apostrophe (') first. For example, for years in column headings, you can enter '1999, '2000 and '2001. The apostrophe is not visible in the cell, it only indicates that the entry is to be recognized as a text. This is useful if, for example, you enter a telephone number or postal code that begins with a zero (0), because a zero (0) at the start of a sequence of digits is removed in normal number formats.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377				0	br	If you have already entered normal numbers in cells and have afterwards changed the format of the cells to "Text", the numbers will remain normal numbers. They will not be converted. Only numbers entered afterwards, or numbers which are then edited, will become text numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728	48			0	br	When numbers are formatted as text, they cannot be used in calculations or formulas.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	br	You can format numbers as text in $[officename] Calc. Open the context menu of a cell or range of cells and choose \<emph\>Format Cells - Numbers\</emph\>, then select "Text" from the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list. Any numbers subsequently entered into the formatted range are interpreted as text. The display of these "numbers" is left-justified, just as with other text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	br	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cell - Numbers\"\>Format - Cell - Numbers\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Formatting Numbers as Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151112				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>cells; rotating text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rotating; text in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; writing vertically\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151112				0	br	\<variable id=\"text_rotate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_rotate.xhp\" name=\"Rotating Text\"\>Rotating Text\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	br	Select the cells whose text you want to rotate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426				0	br	In the \<emph\>Text direction\</emph\> area use the mouse to select in the preview wheel the direction in which the text is to be rotated. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434	6			0	br	In the \<emph\>Text direction \</emph\>area, if you press the \<emph\>ABCD \</emph\>button, the text is written vertically in the direction of the characters.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456				0	br	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05020000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells\"\>Format - Cells\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	br	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05340300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Alignment\"\>Format - Cells - Alignment\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155133				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\>. You will see the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	br	Click the \<emph\>Alignment\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Rotating Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; multi-line\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; text breaks\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>breaks in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>multi-line text in cells\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154346				0	br	\<variable id=\"text_wrap\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_wrap.xhp\" name=\"Writing Multi-line Text\"\>Writing Multi-line Text\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	br	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05020000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cell\"\>Format - Cell\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	br	In \<emph\>Format - Cells - Alignment\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Automatic line break\</emph\> option and click OK.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142				0	br	If you want the text to automatically break at the right border of the cell, proceed as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	br	Select all the cells where you want the text to break at the right border.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	br	Pressing the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter keys inserts a manual line break. This shortcut only works directly in the cell, not in the input line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Writing Multi-line Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155411				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>functions; user-defined\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user-defined functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic IDE for user-defined functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>IDE; Basic IDE\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>programming;functions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147340				0	br	Copying a Function To a Document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260				0	br	Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153305				0	br	Applying a User-defined Function in $[officename] Calc				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411				0	br	\<variable id=\"userdefined_function\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/userdefined_function.xhp\" name=\"Defining Functions Yourself\"\>User-Defined Functions\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145232	19			0	br	In stage 2 of "Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic", in the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> dialog you clicked on \<emph\>Edit \</emph\>. As the default, in the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field the \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> module is selected. The \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> library resides locally in your user directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366				0	br	You can define your own functions using the Basic-IDE. This method requires a basic knowledge of programming.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384				0	br	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>(Name of the Calc document) - Standard - Module1\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3146776				0	br	The function is evaluated and you will see the result in the selected cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148606				0	br	Open a Calc document and enter numbers for the function parameters \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\> in cells A1, B1, and C1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148699				0	br	Paste the clipboard contents in the Basic-IDE of the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148869				0	br	Once you have defined the function \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> in the Basic-IDE, you can apply it the same way as the built-in functions of $[officename] Calc.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043				0	br	Your function is automatically saved in the default module and is now available. If you apply the function in a Calc document that is to be used on another computer, you can copy the function to the Calc document as described in the next section.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150086				0	br	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> and click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	br	Enter the function code. In this example, we define a \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> function that calculates the volume of a rectangular solid with side lengths \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\> and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150517				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	br	You can program functions as \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060111.xhp\" name=\"add-ins\"\>add-ins\</link\>. This method requires an advanced knowledge of programming.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3153969				0	br	You can apply user-defined functions in $[officename] Calc in the following ways:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022				0	br	If you want to copy the user-defined function to a Calc document:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	br	Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button. You will now see the Basic IDE.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155264				0	br	=VOL(A1;B1;C1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	br	Close the Basic-IDE window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019				0	br	Set the cursor in another cell and enter the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430				0	br	In the Basic-IDE, select the source of your user-defined function and copy it to the clipboard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_idN1081D				0	br	Close the Basic-IDE.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	User-Defined Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156442				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>values; limiting on input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>limits; specifying value limits on input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>permitted cell contents\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data validity\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>validity\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; validity\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>error messages; defining for incorrect input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>actions in case of incorrect input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Help tips; defining text for cell input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>notes;help text for cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining input help\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>macros; running when incorrect input\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data; validity check\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155603				0	br	Using Cell Contents Validity				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156442				0	br	\<variable id=\"validity\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/validity.xhp\" name=\"Validity of Cell Contents\"\>Validity of Cell Contents\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	br	The validity rule is activated when a new value is entered. If an invalid value has already been inserted into the cell, or if you insert a value in the cell either with drag-and-drop or by copying and pasting, the validity rule will not take effect.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3147416				0	br	If you select "Stop" as the action, invalid entries are not accepted, and the previous cell contents are retained.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3148586				0	br	After changing the action for a cell on the \<emph\>Error Alert\</emph\> tab page and closing the dialog with OK, you must first select another cell before the change takes effect.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3148837	8			0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Validity\</item\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3149011	35			0	br	To display the error message, select \<emph\>Show error message when invalid values are entered\</emph\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3149317				0	br	Select the next condition under \<emph\>Data\</emph\>. According to what you choose, additional options will be selectable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3149947				0	br	If you select "Macro", then by using the \<emph\>Browse\</emph\> button you can specify a macro to be run in the event of an error.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3150033				0	br	Select "Warning" or "Information" to display a dialog in which the entry can either be canceled or accepted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3151389				0	br	After you have determined the conditions for cell validity, you can use the other two tab pages to create message boxes:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3153011				0	br	If you select "Whole Numbers", values such as "12.5" are not allowed. Choosing "Date" allows date information both in the local date format as well as in the form of a \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03030101.xhp\" name=\"serial date\"\>serial date\</link\>. Similarly, the "Time" condition permits time values such as "12:00" or serial time numbers. "Text Length" stipulates that cells are allowed to contain text only.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3154805				0	br	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12120000.xhp\" name=\"Data - Validity\"\>Data - Validity\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3155959				0	br	Select the cells for which you want to define a new validity rule.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3156020	9			0	br	On the \<emph\>Criteria\</emph\> tab page, enter the conditions for new values entered into cells. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	br	For each cell, you can define entries to be valid. Invalid entries to a cell will be rejected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3156396				0	br	On the \<emph\>Error Alert\</emph\> tab page, select the action to be carried out in the event of an error.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3159208				0	br	In the \<emph\>Allow\</emph\> field, select an option.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id3159261				0	br	On the \<emph\>Input Help\</emph\> tab page, enter the title and the text of the tip, which will then be displayed if the cell is selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id5174718				0	br	You can choose \<emph\>Tools - Detective\</emph\> at any time and choose the command \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06030800.xhp\" name=\"Mark Invalid Data\"\>\<emph\>Mark Invalid Data\</emph\>\</link\> to display which cells contain invalid values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	par_id9224829				0	br	Select "List" to enter a list of valid entries.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\validity.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Validity of Cell Contents				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>names; defining for cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>values; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constants definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;names for cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>addressing; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell names; defining/addressing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id1226233				0	br	Naming cells and formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434				0	br	\<variable id=\"value_with_name\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/value_with_name.xhp\" name=\"Naming Cells\"\>Naming Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id4391918				0	br	Allowed names				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id1120029				0	br	Allowed special characters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2129581				0	br	Names in Calc can contain letters, numeric characters, and some special characters. Names must start with a letter or an underline character.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2816553				0	br	blank ( ) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character, and not for a cell range				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	br	If more than one name starts with the same characters, you can scroll through all the names using the Tab key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153711				0	br	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04070100.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Names - Define\"\>Insert - Names - Define\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153954				0	br	Select a cell or range of cells, then choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	br	Press the Enter key in order to accept the name from the tip.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154942				0	br	If you type the name in a formula, after the first few characters entered you will see the entire name as a tip.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	br	Type the name of the selected area in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> field. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. The newly defined name appears in the list below. Click OK to close the dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id328989				0	br	Names must not be the same as cell references. For example, the name A1 is invalid because A1 is a cell reference to the top left cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3362224				0	br	underline (_) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4769737				0	br	Names for cell ranges must not include blanks. Blanks are allowed within names for single cells, sheets and documents.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4889675				0	br	Use the \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog to define names for formulas or parts of formulas you need more often. In order to specify range names,				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4891506				0	br	period (.) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5489364				0	br	A good way of making the references to cells and cell ranges in formulas legible is to give the ranges names. For example, you can name the range A1:B2 \<emph\>Start\</emph\>. You can then write a formula such as "=SUM(Start)". Even after you insert or delete rows or columns, $[officename] still correctly assigns the ranges identified by name. Range names must not contain any spaces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5774101				0	br	You can also name other cell ranges in this dialog by entering the name in the field and then selecting the respective cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id953398				0	br	For example, it is much easier to read a formula for sales tax if you can write "= Amount * Tax_rate" instead of "= A5 * B12". In this case, you would name cell A5 "Amount" and cell B12 "Tax_rate."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Naming Cells				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>HTML WebQuery\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ranges; inserting in tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>external data; inserting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; inserting external data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>web pages; importing data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>WebQuery filter\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>external data;WebQuery\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; external data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data sources; external data\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863				0	br	\<variable id=\"webquery\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/webquery.xhp\" name=\"Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)\"\>Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146976				0	br	Inserting by Dialog				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149021				0	br	Inserting by Navigator				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149126				0	br	Editing the external data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768				0	br	If you have loaded an HTML document with the \<emph\>Web Page Query\</emph\> filter as the source document, you will find the tables in the Navigator, named continuously from "HTML_table1" onwards, and also two range names that have been created:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Link to External Data\</emph\>. This opens the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\"\>External Data\</link\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	br	You can use the same method to insert ranges defined by name from a Calc or Microsoft Excel spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3148842	20			0	br	In the Navigator select the \<emph\>Insert as link\</emph\> drag mode \<image id=\"img_id3152985\" src=\"sw/imglst/sc20238.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3152985\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	br	In the large list box of the dialog, select the named ranges or tables you want to insert.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	br	The following insert methods are available:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149897				0	br	\<item type=\"literal\"\>HTML_tables\</item\> - designates all HTML tables in the document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	br	Enter the URL of the HTML document or the name of the spreadsheet. Press Enter when finished. Click the \<emph\>...\</emph\> button to open a file selection dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3150205				0	br	In the target document open the Navigator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3152873				0	br	\<item type=\"literal\"\>HTML_all\</item\> - designates the entire document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3152990				0	br	In the lower combo box of the Navigator select the source document. The Navigator now shows the range names and database ranges or the tables contained in the source document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3153965				0	br	Open two documents: the $[officename] Calc spreadsheet in which the external data is to be inserted (target document) and the document from which the external data derives (source document).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	br	Set the cell cursor at the cell where the new content will be inserted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3154650				0	br	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\" name=\"External data dialog\"\>External data dialog\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	br	You can also specify that the ranges or tables are updated every n seconds.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131				0	br	With the help of the \<emph\>Web Page Query ($[officename] Calc)\</emph\> import filter, you can insert tables from HTML documents in a Calc spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	br	The import filter can create names for cell ranges on the fly. As much formatting as possible is retained, while the filter intentionally does not load any images.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3157978				0	br	Drag the desired external data from the Navigator into the target document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3159228				0	br	Open \<emph\>Edit - Links\</emph\>. Here you can edit the link to the external data.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150439				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>years; 2-digits\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>dates; 19xx/20xx\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439				0	br	\<variable id=\"year2000\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/year2000.xhp\" name=\"19xx/20xx Years\"\>19xx/20xx Years\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	br	This means that if you enter a date of 1/1/30 or higher, it will be treated internally as 1/1/1930 or higher. All lower two-digit years apply to the 20xx century. So, for example, 1/1/20 is converted into 1/1/2020.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151116				0	br	The year in a date entry is often entered as two digits. Internally, the year is managed by $[officename] as four digits, so that in the calculation of the difference from 1/1/99 to 1/1/01, the result will correctly be two years.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011				0	br	Under \<emph\>Tools - Options - $[officename] - General\</emph\> you can define the century that is used when you enter a year with only two digits. The default is 1930 to 2029.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	19xx/20xx Years				2002-02-02 02:02:02
