helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	hd_id9204077				0	br	Constraints				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1039155				0	br	The variables for the second variation are calculated as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1328470				0	br	For a category chart (for example a line chart), the regression information is calculated using numbers 1, 2, 3, … as x-values. This is also true if your data series uses other numbers as names for the x-values. For such charts the XY chart type might be more suitable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1664479				0	br	exponential regression: only positive y-values are considered,				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id181279				0	br	You should transform your data accordingly; it is best to work on a copy of the original data and transform the copied data.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id1857661				0	br	 For \<emph\>power regression\</emph\> curves a transformation to a linear model takes place. The power regression follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=b*x^a\</item\> , which is transformed to \<item type=\"literal\"\>ln(y)=ln(b)+a*ln(x)\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id2083498				0	br	Besides m, b and r² the array function \<emph\>LINEST\</emph\> provides additional statistics for a regression analysis.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id2134159				0	br	a = SLOPE(Data_Y;LN(Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id2357249				0	br	r² = RSQ(LN(Data_Y);LN(Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id296334				0	br	A regression curve is shown in the legend automatically.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id33875				0	br	Create a table with the columns x, x², x³, … , xⁿ, y up to the desired degree n. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id394299				0	br	The \<emph\>logarithm regression\</emph\> follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=a*ln(x)+b\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4349192				0	br	To insert a regression curve for a single data series, select the data series in the chart and choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Object Properties - Statistics\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4416638				0	br	a = SLOPE(LN(Data_Y);Data_X) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4562211				0	br	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/04050000.xhp\"\>Statistics tab page\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id4679097				0	br	 For exponential regression curves a transformation to a linear model takes place. The optimal curve fitting is related to the linear model and the results are interpreted accordingly. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5068514				0	br	The first row of the LINEST output contains the coefficients of the regression polynomial, with the coefficient of xⁿ at the leftmost position.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5437177				0	br	r² = RSQ(LN(Data_Y);Data_X) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5649281				0	br	r² = RSQ(Data_Y;LN(Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5676747				0	br	To insert regression curves for all data series double-click the chart to enter edit mode. Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Statistic\</item\>, then select the type of regression curve from No, Linear, Logarithm, Exponential, or Power regression.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id5946531				0	br	b = INTERCEPT(Data_Y ;LN(Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id6637165				0	br	b = INTERCEPT(Data_Y ;Data_X) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id6946317				0	br	Besides m, b and r² the array function LOGEST provides additional statistics for a regression analysis.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7127292				0	br	Calculate the coefficient of determination by				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7184057				0	br	m = EXP(SLOPE(LN(Data_Y);Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7212744				0	br	logarithm regression: only positive x-values are considered,				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7272255				0	br	Regression curves can be added to all chart types except for Pie and Stock charts.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7393719				0	br	 The calculation of the regression curve considers only data pairs with the following values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7735221				0	br	%PRODUCTNAME doesn't show parameters of the regression curve inside the chart. You can calculate the parameters using Calc functions as follows.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id786767				0	br	b = EXP(INTERCEPT(LN(Data_Y);Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7879268				0	br	Calculate the coefficient of determination by				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id7951902				0	br	m = SLOPE(Data_Y;Data_X) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8202154				0	br	The first element of the third row of the LINEST output is the value of r². See the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp#Section8\"\>LINEST\</link\> function for details on proper use and an explanation of the other output parameters.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8398998				0	br	If you insert a regression curve to a chart type that uses categories, like \<emph\>Line \</emph\>or \<emph\>Column, \</emph\>then the numbers 1, 2, 3, \<emph\>…\</emph\> are used as x-values to calculate the regression curve.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8517105				0	br	a = SLOPE(LN(Data_Y);LN(Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8720053				0	br	Use the formula \<item type=\"literal\"\>=LINEST(Data_Y,Data_X)\</item\> with the complete range x to xⁿ (without headings) as Data_X. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8734702				0	br	power regression: only positive x-values and positive y-values are considered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8918729				0	br	A \<emph\>polynomial regression\</emph\> curve cannot be added automatically. You must calculate this curve manually. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id8962065				0	br	If the chart is in edit mode, %PRODUCTNAME gives you the equation of the regression curve and the coefficient of determination R². Click on the regression curve to see the information in the status bar. The \<link href=\"text/shared/guide/active_help_on_off.xhp\"\>extended tip help\</link\> shows the same information when you point to the regression curve.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9112216				0	br	The exponential regression follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=b*exp(a*x)\</item\> or \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=b*m^x\</item\>, which is transformed to \<item type=\"literal\"\>ln(y)=ln(b)+a*x\</item\> or \<item type=\"literal\"\>ln(y)=ln(b)+ln(m)*x\</item\> respectively.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9244361				0	br	r² = RSQ(Data_Y;Data_X) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9251991				0	br	The \<emph\>linear regression\</emph\> follows the equation \<item type=\"literal\"\>y=m*x+b\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9569689				0	br	The regression curve has the same color as the corresponding data series. To change the line properties, select the regression curve and choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Object Properties - Line\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	par_id9827265				0	br	b = EXP(INTERCEPT(LN(Data_Y);LN(Data_X)) 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\04050100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Regression Curves				2002-02-02 02:02:02
