helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166425				0	br	Aligning Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126				0	br	Click the object behind which you want to arrange the selected object.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\> to bring up the context menu and choose one of the arrange options:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3150535				0	br	Selected objects are distributed evenly along the horizontal or vertical axis. The two outermost objects are used as reference points and do not move when the \<emph\>Distribution\</emph\> command is applied.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3151390				0	br	If you select three or more objects in Draw, you can also use the \<link href=\"text/shared/01/05360000.xhp\" name=\"Distribution\"\>\<emph\>Distribution\</emph\>\</link\> command to distribute the vertical and horizontal spacing evenly between the objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994				0	br	The \<emph\>Alignment\</emph\> function enables you to align objects relative to each other or relative to the page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727				0	br	Each object that you place in your document is successively stacked on the preceding object. To re-arrange the stacking order of a selected object proceed as follows.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022				0	br	Shift-click both objects to select them.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\> to open the context menu and choose \<emph\>Behind Object\</emph\>. The mouse pointer changes to a hand.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_id3155114				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Arrange\</item\> to open the context menu and choose \<emph\>Reverse\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107D5				0	br	Click the object that you want to re-arrange.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107E6				0	br	\<emph\>Bring to Front\</emph\> places the object on top of all other objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107EC				0	br	\<emph\>Bring Forward\</emph\> places the object one place forward in the stack of objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107F2				0	br	\<emph\>Send Backward\</emph\> places the object one place back in the stack of objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107F8				0	br	\<emph\>Send to Back\</emph\> places the object behind all other objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN107FE				0	br	\<emph\>Behind Object\</emph\> places the object behind another object that you select				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN10811				0	br	Click the object that you want to re-arrange.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A3				0	br	Select an object to align it to the page or select multiple objects to align them relative to each other.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A7				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Alignment\</item\> and select one of the alignment options.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108AE				0	br	Distributing Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108CE				0	br	Select three or more objects to be distributed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108D2				0	br	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify - Distribution\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	par_idN108DA				0	br	Select the horizontal and vertical distribution option and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\align_arrange.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Arranging, Aligning and Distributing Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149263				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>colors; defining and saving\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user-defined colors\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>custom colors\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149263				0	br	\<variable id=\"color_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/color_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Custom Colors\"\>Defining Custom Colors\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155600				0	br	To define a custom color				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145116				0	br	If you want to add your custom color to the standard color table, enter a name in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> text box and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145236				0	br	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/03170000.xhp\" name=\"Color bar\"\>Color bar\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145386				0	br	Enter a numeric value in the boxes next to the color components. The new color appears in the preview box directly above the color model box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3147244				0	br	If you want to replace the color in the standard color table that your custom color is based on, click \<emph\>Modify\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Format - Area\</emph\> and click the \<emph\>Colors\</emph\> tab. A table of the predefined colors is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152871				0	br	You can also create a color using a color spectrum. Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button to open the \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01010501.xhp\" name=\"Color\"\>\<emph\>Color\</emph\>\</link\> dialog. Click a color and click OK. Use the Hue, Saturation, and Brightness boxes to adjust your color selection.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152987	16			0	br	The RGB color model mixes red, green and blue light to create colors on a computer screen. In the RGB model, the three color components are additive and can have values ranging from 0 (black) to 255 (white). The CMYK color model combines Cyan (C), Magenta (M), Yellow (Y), and blacK (K, also used for "Key") to create colors for printing. The four colors of the CMYK models are subtractive and are defined as percentages. Black corresponds to 100 % and white to 0 %.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152992				0	br	Select the RGB or CMYK color model in the box below the preview boxes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153011				0	br	Do one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154511				0	br	If you want, you can mix a custom color and add it to a color table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154657				0	br	Changes made to the standard color table are permanent and are saved automatically.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3166425				0	br	Click a color in the table that is similar to the one you want to mix. The color appears in the upper preview box to the right of the table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id4979705				0	br	%PRODUCTNAME uses only the RGB color model for printing in color. The CMYK controls are provided only to ease the input of color values using CMYK notation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Defining Custom Colors				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156443				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>combining; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>merging; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>connecting; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; combining\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>intersecting draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>polygons; intersecting/subtracting/merging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subtracting polygons\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constructing shapes\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145144				0	br	Shape Commands				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147370				0	br	Shapes - Intersect				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149950				0	br	Shapes - Merge				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150344				0	br	To combine 2D objects:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153002				0	br	Shapes - Subtract				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156402				0	br	To construct a shape:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156443	64			0	br	\<variable id=\"combine_etc\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/combine_etc.xhp\" name=\"Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes\"\>Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159229				0	br	Constructing Shapes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3145587				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Modify - Combine\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3145829	91			0	br	\<emph\>Merge\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3146978				0	br	Unlike groups, a combined object takes on the properties of the lowermost object in the stacking order. You can split apart combined objects, but the original object properties are lost.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403				0	br	Shape commands only work on 2D objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3148585				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id3145593\" src=\"res/helpimg/formvers.png\" width=\"88.37mm\" height=\"33.34mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3145593\"\>Illustration for merging shapes\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020	65			0	br	Combined drawing objects act as grouped objects, except that you cannot enter the group to edit the individual objects. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150001				0	br	Adds the area of the selected objects to the area of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150022				0	br	Subtracts the area of the selected objects from the area of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150049				0	br	You can construct shapes by applying the \<link href=\"text/simpress/01/13180000.xhp\" name=\"Shapes\"\>\<emph\>Shapes\</emph\>\</link\>\<emph\>- Merge, Subtract and Intersect\</emph\> commands to two or more drawing objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150338				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id3154505\" src=\"res/helpimg/formsubt.png\" width=\"88.11mm\" height=\"33.34mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3154505\"\>Illustration for subtracting shapes\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150539				0	br	Constructed shapes take on the properties of the lowermost object in the stacking order.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150570				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id3150658\" src=\"res/helpimg/formschn.png\" width=\"87.05mm\" height=\"33.07mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150658\"\>Illustratoin for intersecting shapes\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3150650				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Modify - Shapes\</emph\> and one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3151020				0	br	The area outside the overlap is removed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153034				0	br	\<emph\>Intersect\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153249				0	br	In the illustration, the uncombined objects are on the left and the combined objects on the right.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3153931				0	br	In the following illustrations, the original objects are on the left and the modified shapes on the right.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	br	You can only combine 2D objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3154680	92			0	br	\<emph\>Subtract\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3155088				0	br	When you combine objects, holes appear where the objects overlap.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id3157978\" src=\"res/helpimg/kombi1.png\" width=\"212.46mm\" height=\"63.5mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3157978\"\>Illustration for combining objects\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3157874				0	br	Select two or more 2D objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3157972				0	br	The overlapping area of the selected objects creates the new shape.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	par_id3166428				0	br	Select two or more 2D objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\combine_etc.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Combining Objects and Constructing Shapes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150715				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; cross-fading two objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cross-fading; two draw objects\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149209				0	br	To cross-fade two objects:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150715				0	br	\<variable id=\"cross_fading\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/cross_fading.xhp\" name=\"Cross-Fading Two Objects\"\>Cross-Fading Two Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3149405				0	br	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3150370				0	br	Hold down Shift and click each object.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3151240				0	br	A group containing the two original objects and the specified number (increments) of cross-faded objects is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	br	Cross-fading creates shapes and distributes them by uniform increments between two drawing objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3154766				0	br	You can edit the individual objects of a group by selecting the group and pressing F3. Press Ctrl+F3 to exit the group editing mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3155112				0	br	The cross-fading command is only available in $[officename] Draw. You can, however, copy and paste cross-faded objects into $[officename] Impress.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3155760				0	br	\<link href=\"text/simpress/01/02150000.xhp\" name=\"Editing - Cross-fading\"\>Editing - Cross-fading\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3156450				0	br	Enter a value to specify the number of objects between the start and end of the cross-fade in the \<emph\>Increments\</emph\> box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3159203				0	br	\<image id=\"img_id3150210\" src=\"res/helpimg/ueberblenden.png\" width=\"74.88mm\" height=\"65.62mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150210\"\>Illustration for crossfading\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3166428				0	br	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Cross-fading\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Cross-Fading Two Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146974				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>sectors of circles/ellipses\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>segments of circles/ellipses\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>circle segments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ellipses; segments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>drawing; sectors and segments\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146974				0	br	\<variable id=\"draw_sector\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/draw_sector.xhp\" name=\"Drawing Sectors and Segments\"\>Drawing Sectors and Segments\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151075				0	br	To draw a sector of a circle or an ellipse:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3145361				0	br	Release the mouse button when the circle has reached the size you want. A line corresponding to the circle radius appears in the circle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3146874				0	br	Position the pointer where you want to place the second boundary of the sector and click. The completed sector is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396				0	br	The \<emph\>Ellipse\</emph\> toolbar contains tools for drawing ellipses and circles. You can also draw segments and sectors of circles and ellipses.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3148581				0	br	To draw a segment of a circle or ellipse, follow the steps for creating a sector based on a circle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868				0	br	To create a circle by dragging from the center, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Option \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Alt\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> while dragging.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872				0	br	Position the pointer where you want to place the first boundary of the sector and click.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3150199				0	br	Position the pointer at the edge of the circle you want to draw and drag to create the circle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3153084				0	br	To draw an arc based on an ellipse, choose one of the arc icons and follow the same steps for creating a sector based on a circle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335	33			0	br	Open the \<emph\>Ellipses\</emph\> toolbar and click one of the \<emph\>Circle Pie\</emph\> or \<emph\>Ellipse Pie\</emph\> icons \<image id=\"img_id3155768\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_circlepie_unfilled.png\" width=\"5.64mm\" height=\"5.64mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3155768\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>. The mouse pointer changes to a cross hair with a small icon of a sector.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	par_id3157871				0	br	As the radius line that follows the pointer is constrained to the circle boundaries, you can click anywhere in the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\draw_sector.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Drawing Sectors and Segments				2002-02-02 02:02:02
