helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159197				0	br	Exporting				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163804				0	br	\<switchinline select=\"appl\"\>\<caseinline select=\"IMPRESS\"\>Show notes \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145174				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_PERL\"\>Used by WebCast export to create HTML pages and Perl scripts.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145792				0	br	Now, copy all *.htm, *.jpg and *.gif files from the directories that were specified during the export into the //user/local/http/presentation/ directory on your HTTP Server and copy all files with the *.pl and *.txt extensions into the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	br	To export to ASP, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. You then see the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialog in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. Once you have selected a directory and entered a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>. For export as ASP, we recommend selecting a "secret" file name for the HTML file (see below for more details). You then see the \<emph\>HTML Export\</emph\> dialog. Several files will be written to the directory you have just selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146972				0	br	To export, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialog, in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. After selecting a folder and entering a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>HTML Export Wizard\</emph\>. This will write some files to the folder you have just selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147088				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_STANDARD\"\>Creates standard HTML pages from export pages.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147313				0	br	In this example, you have a Linux computer with an HTTP server. The URL of your HTTP server is http://myserver.com and the output directory of your HTML documents is the //user/local/http/ directory. Your Perl scripts are contained in the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. Enter secret.htm as an export file name and presentation.htm as \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\>. In the \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\> text box enter http://myserver.com/presentation/ and for the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> enter http://myserver.com/cgi-bin/.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340				0	br	You should first move the files that have been created during the export into the correct directory on the HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148432				0	br	In the second page of the HTML Export, select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as the publication type.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148479				0	br	Once you have finished installing the Perl files, the presenter will be able to give the presentation. The listeners can view this presentation under the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/presentation.htm.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148922	28			0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_ASP\"\>When you select the\<emph\> ASP \</emph\>option, the WebCast export creates ASP pages. Note that the HTML presentation can only be offered by a web server supporting ASP.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018				0	br	In the option area for WebCast, select \<emph\>Perl\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149203				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_INDEX\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative) to be entered by the viewer in order to see the presentation.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_KIOSK\"\>Creates a default HTML presentation as a kiosk export, in which the slides are automatically advanced after a specified amount of time.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149410				0	br	The presenter uses the entered file name to change between the slides viewed by the audience. You can save the WebCast files locally or save them directly to an HTTP server. You can later transfer locally saved files to the HTTP server by FTP. Note that WebCast only works if the files are requested over an HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149655				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_ENDLESS\"\>Automatically restarts the HTML presentation after the last slide has been displayed.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149910				0	br	You can use WebCast as soon as the exported files can be accessed from an HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CHG_DEFAULT\"\>The slide transition depends on the timing that you set for each slide in the presentation. If you set a manual page transition, the HTML presentation introduces a new page by pressing any key from your keyboard.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150543				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_WEBCAST\"\>In a WebCast export, automatic scripts will be generated with Perl or ASP support.\</ahelp\> This enables the speaker (for example, a speaker in a telephone conference using a slide show in the Internet) to change the slides in the audience's web browsers. You will find more information on \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01110200.xhp\" name=\"WebCast\"\>WebCast\</link\> later in this section.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	br	You have saved the files that have been created during the Export process in the c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation\\ directory. In this directory, the Export creates an HTML file that can be named, for example, as "secret.htm". You entered this name in the Save dialog (see above). The presenter can now browse to the HTML Export files by entering the http://myserver.com/presentation/secret.htm URL in any HTTP Browser having JavaScript support. The presenter is now able to modify the page using some form controls.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151014				0	br	All files having the pl and txt extensions have to be moved into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> text box. This directory has to be configured in a way that the Perl scripts contained there can also be run by an HTTP request.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151245				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:TIMEFIELD:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_DURATION_TMF\"\>Defines the amount of time for each slide display.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151250				0	br	On UNIX systems grant the files with the pl extension the rights to be executable by the HTTP server. Normally, this is done with the chmod command. The rights of the currpic.txt file must be set to be writable by the HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151330				0	br	You can specify if you want to include frames, create a title, or display presentation notes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152375				0	br	Let's assume that you installed the Microsoft Internet Information Server on your computer. You entered the "c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation" directory as an HTML output directory during the IIS setup. The URL of your computer is assumed as follows: "http://myserver.com".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	br	There are two possible options for exporting $[officename] Impress presentations using WebCast technology: Active Server Pages (ASP) and Perl.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152780				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CONTENT\"\>Creates a title page for your document.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153126				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CHG_AUTO\"\>The page transition takes place automatically after the specified period of time elapses and does not depend on the presentation's contents\</ahelp\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	br	Select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as a publishing type on the second page of the HTML Export Wizard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153364				0	br	In either case, the WebCast needs an HTTP server offering either Perl or ASP as scripting. Therefore, the exporting option depends on the HTTP server used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153534				0	br	Now you should be able to use WebCast.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153920				0	br	Login on your server as root and switch to the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. You can define the corresponding rights using the chmod command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154094				0	br	Determines the type of publication.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154503				0	br	\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154509				0	br	In the \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\> text box, enter the file name of the HTML document that will be used by the audience. In \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>, enter the URL of the directory that will be used for the presentation and, in \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\>, enter the URL for the CGI script directory. You can now define further settings on the following pages of the Wizard or start the export process by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154751				0	br	Defines the basic settings for the intended export.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154790				0	br	In the options area for WebCast, select the \<emph\>Active Server Pages (ASP)\</emph\> option. You can now continue defining other settings or start the export by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154824				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_FRAMES\"\>Creates standard HTML pages with frames. The exported page will be placed in the main frame, and the frame to the left will display a table of contents in the form of hyperlinks.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155936				0	br	Move all files having the htm, jpg and gif extensions into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the text box \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156214				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_URL\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative), where the created HTML presentation on the web server has been saved.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156362				0	br	The entered file name will be used by the presenter to switch through the slides. Please select an empty directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156711	66			0	br	\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_NOTES\"\>Specifies that your notes are also displayed.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157974				0	br	Do not use the same directory for two different HTML exports.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159110				0	br	The audience can now view the slide selected by the presenter through the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/webcast.asp. They cannot move to other slides found at this URL, unless the file names are known. Please ensure that the HTTP server does not show the directory listing.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159255				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CGI\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative) for the generated Perl scripts.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159268				0	br	The files that have been created during the export must now be set up in the Perl enabled HTTP server. This cannot be done automatically because of the variety of different HTTP servers having Perl support. The steps to follow will be described next. Please refer to your server manual or ask your network administrator how to apply these steps on your server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	HTML Export - Page 2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id2298959				0	br	PNG - Portable Network Graphics format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147618				0	br	GIF - Graphics Interchange Format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148552				0	br	Monitor resolution				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149763				0	br	Quality				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151381				0	br	Export sounds when slide advances				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152361				0	br	Low resolution (640x480 Pixel)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153323				0	br	\<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01110300.xhp\" name=\"HTML Export - Page 3\"\>HTML Export - Page 3\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153361				0	br	Medium resolution (800x600 Pixel)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153968				0	br	High resolution (1024x768 Pixel)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154306				0	br	JPG - Compressed file format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154946				0	br	Export				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155341				0	br	Save graphics as				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147102				0	br	Specifies the graphics type and the target screen resolution.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3148947				0	br	Defines the resolution for the target screen. Depending on the selected resolution, the image will be displayed in a reduced size. You can specify a reduction of up to 80% from the original size.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149810				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_RESOLUTION_3\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Select a high resolution for a high quality slide display.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150449				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_SLD_SOUND\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Specifies that the sound files that are defined as an effect for slide transitions are exported.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151384				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:COMBOBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_QUALITY\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Specifies the compression factor of the JPEG graphic.\</ahelp\> A 100% value offers the best quality for a large data range. The 25% factor indicates small files with inferior image quality.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_RESOLUTION_1\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Select the low resolution to keep the file size small, even for presentations with many slides.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153665				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_JPG\" visibility=\"visible\"\>The files are exported as JPEG files.\</ahelp\> JPEG files are compressed, with adjustable compression and can contain more than 256 colors.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154686				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_RESOLUTION_2\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Select the medium resolution for a medium-sized presentation.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154860				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE3_GIF\" visibility=\"visible\"\>The files are exported as GIF files\</ahelp\> GIF files are compressed without loss of data, and have a maximum of 256 colors.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909				0	br	Determines the image format. You can also define the compression value for the export.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110300.xhp	0	help	par_id4653767				0	br	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The files are exported as PNG files. PNG files are compressed without loss of data, and can contain more than 256 colors.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
