helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3148563				0	br	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01010200.xhp\" name=\"Tools - Options - Load/Save - General\"\>\<emph\>Tools - Options - Load/Save - General\</emph\>\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3148672				0	br	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/01070000.xhp\" name=\"Save As\"\>Save As\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3148685				0	br	You can change the backup directory by choosing \<emph\>Tools - Options - $[officename] - Paths\</emph\>, then change the \<emph\>Backups\</emph\> path in the dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415				0	br	The backup copy has the same name as the document, but the extension is .BAK. If the backup folder already contains such a file, it will be overwritten without warning.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3149797	6			0	br	If the \<emph\>Always create backup copy\</emph\> option is selected, the old version of the file is saved to the backup directory whenever you save the current version of the file. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3152780				0	br	Choose \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01010200.xhp\" name=\"Tools - Options - Load/Save - General\"\>\<emph\>Tools - Options - Load/Save - General\</emph\>\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3153526				0	br	This command saves the information necessary to restore the current document in case of a crash. Additionally, in case of a crash %PRODUCTNAME tries automatically to save AutoRecovery information for all open documents, if possible.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\doc_autosave.xhp	0	help	par_id3154760				0	br	Mark \<emph\>Save AutoRecovery information every\</emph\> and select the time interval.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
