helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156384				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>aligning; characters in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; manually aligning\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	br	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156384				0	br	\<variable id=\"align\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/align.xhp\" name=\"Manually Aligning Formula Parts\"\>Manually Aligning Formula Parts\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229				0	br	Empty character strings are a simple way to ensure that texts and formulas are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Make sure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249				0	br	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and character strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	br	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809				0	br	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912				0	br	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one less character is entered in the upper line:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Manually Aligning Formula Parts				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145792				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; changing in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>font attributes;changing defaults\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;changing default attributes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;changing default formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;default formatting\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145792				0	br	\<variable id=\"attributes\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/attributes.xhp\" name=\"Changing Default Attributes\"\>Changing Default Attributes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484				0	br	Can default formats in $[officename] Math be modified?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231				0	br	a + b				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870				0	br	Some parts of formulas are always formatted bold or italic by default.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872				0	br	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210				0	br	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810				0	br	nitalic a + bold b.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
