helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596				0	br	\<variable id=\"brackets\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/brackets.xhp\" name=\"Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\"\>Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511				0	br	Inserting fractions into formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3083280				0	br	{a + c} over 2 = m				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	br	In the case of a fraction whose numerator and denominator consist of a product, a sum, and so on, the values that belong together must be bracketed together.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021				0	br	Use the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297				0	br	m = {a + c} over 2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3154703				0	br	or				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Merging Formula Parts in Brackets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155961				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>comments; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;comments in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	br	How does one attach comments that don't appear in the document to a formula?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155961				0	br	\<variable id=\"comment\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/comment.xhp\" name=\"Entering Comments\"\>Entering Comments\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_id3149499				0	br	A comment begins with a double percent sign \<emph\>%%\</emph\>, and extends to the next line-end character (Enter key). Everything that lies in between is ignored and is not printed out. If there are percent signs in the text, they are treated as part of the text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	br	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D3				0	br	a^2+b^2=c^2 %% Pythagorean theorem.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Entering Comments				2002-02-02 02:02:02
