helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153415				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; inserting in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;brackets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>distances between brackets\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	br	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math, can brackets be shown separately so that the distance between them is freely definable?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153415				0	br	\<variable id=\"parentheses\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/parentheses.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Brackets\"\>Inserting Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3083281				0	br	You can set individual brackets using "left" and "right", but the distance between the brackets will not be fixed, as they adapt to the argument. Nevertheless, there is a way to display brackets so that the distance between them is fixed. To accomplish this, place a "\\" (backslash) before the normal brackets. These brackets now behave like any other symbol and the alignment is the same as with other symbols:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3148489				0	br	size *2 langle x rangle				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3150344				0	br	size *2 \\langle x \\rangle				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960				0	br	left lbrace x right none				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	tit				0	br	Inserting Brackets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155962				0	br	\<bookmark_value\>text strings; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>direct text; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;text in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id5676442				0	br	\<variable id=\"text\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/text.xhp\" name=\"Entering Text\"\>Entering Text\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	br	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	br	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	br	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	br	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	br	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	br	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id755943				0	br	If you have set up Math to convert imported MathType formulas (in Tools - Options - Load/Save - Microsoft Office), you see the formula with a placeholder instead of the asterisk.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	br	W rSup { size 8{*} }				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	br	Some formulas start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
